%N A349774 Numbers that start a run of four consecutive triangular numbers with four prime factors (counted with multiplicity).
%C A349774 As bigomega(n * (n+1) / 2) = bigomega(n) + bigomega(n+1) - 1 we may look at consecutive integers whose total number of prime factors counted with multiplicity is 4 + 1 = 5. If there are 4 such consecutive pairs starting at k then k*(k + 1)/2 is in the sequence. - _David A. Corneth_, Aug 04 2026
%H A349774 David A. Corneth, <a href="/A349774/b349774.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a> (first 5000 terms from Robert Israel)
%e A349774 a(1) = 161596 because 161596 is the smallest number in the first set of four consecutive triangular numbers with four prime factors (counted with multiplicity), i.e., (161596 = 2*2*71*569, 162165 = 3*5*19*569, 162735 = 3*5*19*571, 163306 = 2*11*13*571).
%p A349774 f:= proc(n)
%p A349774    if andmap(t -> NumberTheory:-Omega(t) = 4, [n*(n+1)/2, (n+1)*(n+2)/2, (n+2)*(n+3)/2, (n+3)*(n+4)/2]) then n*(n+1)/2 fi
%p A349774 end proc:
%p A349774 map(f, [$1..10^5]); # _Robert Israel_, Aug 04 2026
%t A349774 t[n_] := n*(n + 1)/2; q[n_] := PrimeOmega[n] == 4; Select[Partition[t /@ Range[35000], 4, 1], AllTrue[#, q] &][[;; , 1]] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Nov 29 2021 *)
%o A349774 (PARI) first(n) = {my(res = List(), prevb = 0, streak = 0); for(i = 2, oo, b = bigomega(i); if(b + prevb == 5, streak++; if(streak >= 4, listput(res, (i-4)*(i-3)/2); if(#res >= n, return(res))), streak = 0); prevb = b); res} \\ _David A. Corneth_, Aug 04 2026
%Y A349774 Cf. A000217, A128905, A348185, A349773.
%K A349774 nonn
